In the beginning, two rivers flowed from opposite mountains—the River of Silver and the River of Gold. Each river possessed life-giving waters, but alone, neither could sustain the valley below.

The Silver River's guardian, Luna, approached the Gold River's guardian, Sol, with a proposal. "If I send my waters toward your river, they will triple in strength when they merge. But you must send some back, or my mountain will wither."

Sol considered the offer warily. "How do I know you will continue sending waters if I return some? You might withhold everything next season."

"How do I know you will return anything at all?" Luna replied. "I must send first, taking all the risk."

They stood at an impasse until Luna said, "Let us try, season by season. What we create together will be greater than what we hoard alone."

In the first season, Luna sent half her waters. Sol, humbled by this gesture, returned a fair portion. The valley bloomed magnificently.

But as seasons passed, each guardian faced the same choice: trust and reciprocate, or withhold and protect what was theirs. The valley's fate rested on their repeated decisions.
